  "key_finding": "Marginal value of one more subject collapses twentyfold across the observed range, to +0.0008 balanced accuracy at the current cohort size. The correlation classifier is fully saturated at +0.0001. Sample size is not the binding constraint; the remaining 0.19 gap to a perfect decoder is a representation and alignment problem.",
  "extrapolation": {
    "model": "accuracy = A - B*n^(-c) fitted to linear_svm independent",
    "n_100": 0.8436,
    "n_200": 0.8707,
    "n_500": 0.8962,
    "caution": "Indicative only. Power-law extrapolation beyond the observed range is unreliable, the fitted asymptote of 0.95 is unsupported, and the fit is driven by the steep small-n points. The marginal-gain table is the robust result."
  },
  "consequences": [
    "Inter-subject normalization and functional alignment are worth more than more data.",
    "An external cohort remains valuable for generalization claims but should not be pursued expecting higher accuracy; the manuscript must not conflate these.",
    "QC-60 removes two subjects, worth about 0.0016 by this curve; any larger difference is about WHICH subjects, not how many."
  ],
